      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every summer, the town hosts a lively Cherry Festival, while autumn brings the Wine Country Harvest Festival to celebrate local vineyards.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_East%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "North East was incorporated as a borough in 1834, after earlier names like Lower Greenfield, Burgettstown, and Gibsonville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_East%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fruit growing has been a cornerstone of the local economy since the 1800s, with cherries and grapes still thriving today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_East%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s scenic setting on Lake Erie’s shore is surrounded by more than 30,000 acres of vineyards, the largest grape‑growing region east of the Rockies.",
